WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.890
From problem to plan: objectives, activities and project logic.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.740
This is the video for module two of Proposal Writing.

3
00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:19.590
The problem statement is the foundation of your proposal. Your objectives must fix the problem you describe, and your activities must tackle its causes.

4
00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:28.910
Who is affected? Be specific. Women aged 25 to 50 who trade in Lafia's main market, not women in Africa.

5
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.640
Where, and how many? Name the community and give numbers you can support.

6
00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:39.710
What is the problem, in measurable terms like income or cost?

7
00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.920
And why does it happen? Name the root causes your project will tackle.

8
00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:53.530
A common mistake is calling the lack of your solution the problem. No soap-making centre is not the problem. Low income is.

9
00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:11.270
Your own data is often the most convincing evidence, because national figures rarely describe your ward. For example, Amaka surveyed 40 members of her cooperative in one week, with a one-page paper form. The course uses illustrative results.

10
00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:17.150
29 of the 40 earned less than 20,000 naira profit a month.

11
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.550
34 had no production skill.

12
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:32.680
26 named lack of training as the main barrier, and 22 named starting capital. So her project will tackle those two root causes: skills and starter capital.

13
00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:38.770
Use figures exactly as your source gives them. Never invent or round up a statistic.

14
00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:49.470
Objectives are the specific changes your project will achieve and measure. Empower women economically sounds good, but it has no number and no date.

15
00:01:50.720 --> 00:02:00.750
SMART means specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound. Amaka writes: by month 3, 40 women complete 12 days of training.

16
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:10.870
By month 6, at least 30 sell their own products at least twice a month, and at least 25 report a monthly profit increase of 10,000 naira or more.

17
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:16.490
Be realistic, not impressive. Meeting a modest target builds a funder's trust.

18
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:20.210
Write each objective at the top of a page.

19
00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:27.780
Under it, list every activity, starting with a verb: recruit, buy, train, mentor, survey, report.

20
00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:36.280
Give each activity one responsible person. For example, the trainers deliver 12 training days, 2 per week.

21
00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:47.710
Mark when each starts and ends, and draw it as a simple chart with activities as rows and months as columns. Avoid busy festive or planting seasons.

22
00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:56.550
Then check that every activity has a budget line, and every budget line has an activity. Reviewers often find gaps here.

23
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:06.440
Funders want to see why your activities should lead to the change you promise. Inputs are your resources. Activities are what you do with them.

24
00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:12.430
Outputs are what you deliver, fully under your control, like 40 women trained.

25
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:20.720
Outcomes are the change that results, like women selling and earning more. Do not put women trained at both levels.

26
00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:28.520
Impact is the long-term change you contribute to. The further along the chain, the less control you have.

27
00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:41.840
A theory of change is a chain of if, then statements. If 40 women get practical training, then they will have the skills to make good products. Assumption: they can attend 2 sessions a week.

28
00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:50.750
If they receive starter kits, then they can produce without borrowing. Assumption: materials stay close to budget prices.

29
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.820
If they get mentoring and market days, then they will sell regularly.

30
00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:04.540
If they sell and track costs, then their income will increase. Trainers turning up is not an assumption. That is your job.

31
00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:13.640
A logframe puts the same logic into a table: goal, outcome, outputs and activities. Read across each row.

32
00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:23.330
It needs a clear summary, one to three indicators with targets, a real source of evidence such as attendance sheets or surveys, and honest assumptions.

33
00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.090
Use the funder's template if there is one.

34
00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:40.290
Your practice. Draw a problem tree and collect at least one piece of evidence. Write a problem statement of 200 to 300 words. Write 2 or 3 SMART objectives with a simple workplan chart.

35
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.870
Then write your if-then chain and fill a logframe. Now continue with the lessons below.
